}

fn add(x: &i32, y: &i32) -> i32 {
    x.wrapping_add(y)
}
```
This program declares two integer variables `x` and `y`, and assigns them the values of 5 and 7, respectively. The `add` function takes two references to `i32` values (`x` and `y`) and returns the result of adding them, which is also an `i32`. The `wrapping_add` method is used to handle the possibility of the result overflowing and wrapping around to a negative value.

Note that this program uses ownership semantics, which means that the variables `x` and `y` are owned by the program, and the `add` function takes ownership of the references to them. This ensures that the variables are correctly cleaned up when they go out of scope and prevent memory leaks.
